[{"location":"#UsingAll","page":"Home","title":"UsingAll","text":"","category":"section"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"Documentation for UsingAll.","category":"page"},{"location":"","page":"Home","title":"Home","text":"","category":"page"},{"location":"#UsingAll.@useall-Tuple{Any}","page":"Home","title":"UsingAll.@useall","text":"@useall SomeModule\n\n@useall is the equivalent of Python's from X import *, and is intended to be used only for debugging and developing purposes,  and not as part of any packages (really, please don't!)\n\nCalling @useall MyModule will make all names (internal and external) in MyModule available, mimicking being inside the package.  This can be useful when debugging code to avoid using qualified names, or when developing new functions in the REPL belonging to a package for quick  prototyping (note that qualifying with MyModule.foo is still required to  overload methods)\n\n\n\n\n\n","category":"macro"}]
